      content:
          '**Diagnosis Confirmation:** AIDP variant of Guillain-Barré syndrome, recovery phase (8 weeks post-onset), with residual severe weakness, neuropathic pain, and autonomic dysfunction.\n\n**EMG Prognostic Factors (Critical for Recovery Prediction):**\n- **Favorable prognostic indicators:** Primarily demyelinating pattern (prolonged distal latencies, conduction block, temporal dispersion, prolonged F-waves) with preserved CMAP amplitudes — suggests remyelination potential with good recovery.\n- **Unfavorable prognostic indicators:** Low distal CMAP amplitudes (<20% of lower limit of normal), inexcitable nerves, widespread denervation on needle EMG — suggests axonal loss requiring slower reinnervation.\n- **Mixed pattern:** Most AIDP patients have secondary axonal degeneration. Recovery depends on the ratio of demyelination to axonal loss.\n- **Timeline markers:** If CMAPs remain absent at 3 months, prognosis for complete recovery is poor. Early CMAP recovery (by 3 months) predicts good functional outcomes.\n\n**GBS Variants to Consider:**\n- **AIDP (this patient):** Most common in North America. Demyelinating. Best recovery potential.\n- **AMAN (acute motor axonal neuropathy):** Pure motor, axonal. Associated with Campylobacter and anti-GM1 antibodies. Can have rapid recovery or prolonged course.\n- **AMSAN:** Motor and sensory axonal. Worst prognosis.\n- **Miller Fisher:** Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia. Anti-GQ1b antibodies. Excellent recovery.\n\n**CIDP Surveillance:**\n- Monitor for treatment-related fluctuations (TRF): deterioration within 8 weeks of IVIg suggesting CIDP rather than GBS\n- If patient deteriorates after initial improvement, or improves then worsens 3+ times, reclassify as CIDP\n- CIDP requires ongoing immunotherapy (IVIg or plasma exchange maintenance)\n- Re-evaluate at 3-month and 6-month marks for any progressive or relapsing course',
