          '**Q: At week 10, the patient develops new weakness in his arms after two weeks of improvement. Should you treat this as a GBS relapse or CIDP?**\n\n**A:** This clinical scenario requires urgent differentiation:\n\n**GBS treatment-related fluctuation (TRF):** Occurs in approximately 10% of GBS patients. Defined as clinical worsening within 8 weeks of IVIg or plasma exchange. Thought to represent ongoing immune attack outlasting the initial treatment. Management: **re-treat with IVIg** (same 0.4 g/kg x 5 days protocol). Typically one episode, self-limited.\n\n**CIDP reclassification criteria:** If the patient experiences 3 or more fluctuations, or continues to worsen beyond 8 weeks from onset, CIDP must be considered. CIDP requires ongoing maintenance immunotherapy.\n\n**Immediate steps:**\n1. Repeat EMG/NCS to look for new demyelination or conduction block compared to prior study\n2. Repeat CSF analysis if considering CIDP\n3. Re-treat with IVIg given he is within 10 weeks of onset — this is likely TRF\n4. Monitor closely for respiratory decline (serial FVC measurements every 6 hours initially)\n5. If improvement after retreatment followed by another decline, strong suspicion for CIDP — initiate maintenance IVIg every 4 weeks\n6. Consider plasma exchange if IVIg retreatment fails\n\nThis clinical scenario highlights why GBS patients in rehab need ongoing neuromuscular surveillance — the physiatrist must be vigilant for immune reactivation.',
