          '**Intensive Inpatient Rehabilitation Program:**\n- PT 2x daily: progressive mobility training, tilt table for orthostatic reconditioning, seated and standing balance, pre-gait activities, bilateral AFO fitting and gait training\n- OT 2x daily: ADL retraining, fine motor recovery exercises, adaptive equipment (built-up utensils, button hook), energy conservation strategies\n- Focus on proximal-to-distal recovery pattern — prioritize proximal strengthening while monitoring for distal nerve recovery\n\n**Orthotic Prescription:**\n- Bilateral custom-molded AFOs for foot drop — start with posterior leaf spring design for lightweight support during gait training\n- Consider carbon fiber AFOs once ambulatory for more dynamic energy return\n- Serial reassessment: as ankle dorsiflexion recovers, transition to lighter orthoses and eventually discontinue\n- Wrist cock-up splints for nighttime to maintain wrist extension positioning\n\n**Neuropathic Pain Management:**\n- Gabapentin: titrate from 300 mg TID to 600-900 mg TID as tolerated (monitor sedation and balance effects)\n- If inadequate: add duloxetine 30-60 mg daily (dual benefit for pain and mood)\n- Topical lidocaine 5% patches to feet for localized allodynia\n- Avoid opioids — will worsen constipation and impair rehabilitation participation\n- Non-pharmacological: TENS unit, desensitization techniques, sleep hygiene optimization\n\n**Fatigue Management (Major GBS Issue):**\n- Fatigue in GBS is disproportionate to weakness level and persists long-term in up to 80% of patients\n- Energy conservation education: pacing, prioritization, positioning\n- Structured rest periods between therapy sessions\n- Graded exercise: avoid overexertion which can worsen fatigue paradoxically\n- Screen for depression as contributor to fatigue\n- Consider low-dose amantadine if fatigue is debilitating and refractory\n\n**Autonomic Management:**\n- Orthostatic hypotension: compression stockings, abdominal binder, gradual position changes, hydration, salt supplementation. Consider midodrine 5 mg TID if refractory.\n- Constipation: bowel program with scheduled docusate + senna, adequate hydration, fiber supplementation\n- Monitor for cardiac dysrhythmias (autonomic GBS involvement) — telemetry if resting HR consistently >120 or irregular',
