          '**Q: The patient is making good progress but hits a plateau at week 12 of rehab. He can walk 100 feet with bilateral AFOs and a rolling walker but is not improving further. What is your approach?**\n\n**A:** A plateau at 12 weeks of rehab (20 weeks post-onset) is not unexpected in GBS but requires systematic evaluation:\n\n1. **Rule out treatable causes:** Repeat EMG to assess for ongoing denervation vs reinnervation. Check for CIDP conversion. Screen for depression (major cause of functional plateau). Evaluate nutritional status and sleep quality.\n\n2. **Reassess rehabilitation intensity:** Consider whether current program intensity is appropriate — GBS patients can be **overexercised**, leading to paradoxical functional decline. Eccentric overloading of partially reinnervated muscles causes damage. Reduce high-intensity resistance and focus on **submaximal, high-repetition functional training**.\n\n3. **Modify goals and timeline:** Reframe the plateau as a consolidation phase, not an endpoint. GBS motor recovery continues for 12-18 months. Transition from acute rehab to intensive outpatient rehabilitation (3-5 days/week).\n\n4. **Technology augmentation:** Body-weight supported treadmill training for gait endurance. Functional electrical stimulation (FES) cycling for lower extremity strengthening and cardiovascular conditioning.\n\n5. **Community reintegration focus:** Shift goals from impairment-based (strength) to participation-based (return to teaching, community mobility, driving).\n\n6. **Discharge planning:** He is appropriate for discharge home with outpatient rehab. Ensure home modifications (stair rail, shower bench), DME (rolling walker, wheelchair for community distances), and home exercise program.',
