      content:
          '**Scenario:** During a therapy session, the patient stops exercising, puts his head down, and says: "I ran my last marathon in 3 hours and 15 minutes. Now I can\'t walk to the bathroom. Who am I if I can\'t run? How long until I can run a marathon again?"\n\n**This Requires Navigating Identity Loss — Not Just Prognosis:**\n\n**Step 1 — Validate the grief:** "I hear you, and I want you to know that what you are feeling is completely understandable. Running was not just exercise for you — it was part of who you are, your community, your identity. Losing that, even temporarily, is a real loss, and it makes sense that you are grieving it."\n\n**Step 2 — Honest prognosis without destroying hope:**\n"Here is what I can honestly tell you about recovery from Guillain-Barré:\n- Most people with your condition do regain the ability to walk independently — that is the most likely outcome.\n- Recovery of nerve function continues for 12 to 18 months, sometimes longer.\n- Your athletic baseline and fitness level before this illness are actually advantages — your cardiovascular system and your brain\'s motor patterns are intact. Your nerves need to heal.\n- Returning to competitive marathon running is a possibility I cannot guarantee. Some GBS survivors do return to high-level athletics, but many experience lasting fatigue and endurance limitations.\n- Setting a marathon as the ultimate goal is fine, but we need intermediate milestones first — walking to the bathroom, walking the hallway, walking outside, walking a mile."\n\n**Step 3 — Reframe identity without dismissing it:**\n"You said \'who am I if I can\'t run?\' I think you are still the same person — disciplined, competitive, goal-oriented. Those qualities are exactly what will drive your recovery. Right now, your marathon is getting from this bed to the end of the hallway. That takes the same mental toughness."\n\n**Step 4 — Connect to peer support:**\n"I would like to connect you with the GBS/CIDP Foundation. They have a peer mentorship program with people who have been exactly where you are now — including some who returned to running. Hearing from someone who has been through it can be more powerful than anything I can say."\n\n**Step 5 — Screen for clinical depression:**\n"I also want to check in on how you are doing emotionally overall — not just about running, but sleep, appetite, motivation, any feelings of hopelessness. What you are going through would be difficult for anyone, and getting support for the emotional side is just as important as the physical therapy."',
